From: quickjs-ng register-VM work
Subject: AOT: call sites and source positions (P6)

What this does
  Adds the engine side of the P6 call-site lowerings and the 2.4 profile
  source positions.

  R1 prototype-epoch guard: a data write to an existing slot of a prototype
  object now bumps ic_watchpoint_epoch, so a prototype method/global value
  folded at bind time is invalidated when the prototype changes. A counter
  rt->aot_epoch_bumps records the bumps and js_aot_stats_dump reports it.
  qjs_aot_proto_fold folds a prototype read to a bind-time value and re-checks
  the epoch (including a saturation guard at JS_IC_EPOCH_MAX). The fold is also
  keyed on the receiver shape it came from: the two-way PIC can swap its primary
  way between two prototype holders that share a shape (e.g. A.prototype.m and
  B.prototype.m), and the epoch does not change on such a read, so without the
  receiver-shape key a fold of A.prototype.m would be served for a B receiver.

  Per-runtime site cache: rt->aot_sites, with aot_site_ensure/aot_site_store,
  caches each call site's callee object and descriptor (bytecode digest, or
  C function pointer + magic + cproto) per runtime rather than in a C static.

  Direct compiled call: qjs_aot_call_b builds the callee frame exactly as
  JS_CallInternal's no-copy-arguments path does and hands it to
  js_regvm_execute, which dispatches straight to the callee's AOT entry.
  Restricted to a non-lazy JS_FUNC_NORMAL bytecode callee compiled in the
  unit, with uncaptured arguments and argc >= arg_count; otherwise it returns
  0 and the caller falls back to the step engine. Exceptions cross back as -1.

  Direct native C call: qjs_aot_call_c identity-guards the site, calls f_f and
  f_f_f on unboxed doubles through the bound pointer, and routes
  generic/generic_magic through js_call_c_function (realm, arg padding). The
  profile's cproto guards the specialisation.

  Source positions (2.4): regvm_encode_and_verify records one int32 source pc
  per register-VM byte into a regvm_src_pc[] side table carried on
  JSFunctionBytecode; aot_dump_one prints src=<line>:<col> on every L/R/S/C
  profile line via find_line_num.

Why we need it
  Without a callee identity guard a cached call target can go stale when the
  callee register or a prototype method changes. Without the epoch bump a
  folded prototype method would keep calling the old function after the
  prototype is written. The direct compiled call removes the generic call
  dispatch for the common case where the callee is compiled in the same unit.
  The source positions let a profile consumer map a hot site back to the JS
  source.

What it adds
  quickjs.c
      qjs_aot_call_b; qjs_aot_call_c; aot_site_ensure/aot_site_store;
      qjs_aot_proto_fold; the is_prototype data-write bump; rt->aot_epoch_bumps
      and its report; regvm_src_pc[] plumbing and aot_src_str.
  quickjs-aot-rt.h
      qjs_aot_call_b declaration.

  Nothing here is compiled without -DQJS_REGVM; QJS_AOT is off by default.
  Box2D checksum 546.1008391043414, test262-fast 50/81192, diff 398/0, ASan
  clean. Mutants AOT_MUT_SKIP_CALLEE_GUARD, AOT_MUT_NO_ARG_PAD and
  AOT_MUT_EPOCH_NO_DATA_WRITE_BUMP each turn a kernel red.

